## Yes – you can draw contour lines **directly from a `raster-dem` source at runtime**  
MapLibre GL JS does not have a built-in “contour” layer type, but the **`maplibre-contour`** library does the heavy lifting for you:

* It reads the same DEM tiles you already use for hillshade / 3-D terrain.  
* In a Web-Worker it extracts elevation values, runs a marching-squares algorithm and emits **vector tiles** that contain the contour geometry.  
* Those vector tiles are then added to the map like any other `vector` source and styled with a normal `line` (or `symbol`) layer.

Below is a step-by-step guide, a complete working example, and a few gotchas to keep in mind.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the library

```bash
# npm / yarn / pnpm
npm install maplibre-contour
# or, if you prefer a CDN:
# <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-contour@latest/dist/maplibre-contour.min.js"></script>
```

> **Why a worker?**  
> DEM decoding and contour generation are CPU-intensive. `maplibre-contour` runs the algorithm in a Web-Worker by default (`worker: true`). If you are on a very low-end device you can set `worker: false` to run on the main thread, but expect a noticeable UI freeze.

---

## 2️⃣ Create a **DEM source** that `maplibre-contour` can read

The library needs a *plain* `raster-dem` source definition (the same you would use for hillshade or `setTerrain`).  
Make sure the **`encoding`** matches the tiles you are serving (`terrarium`, `mapbox`, `raw`, …).  

```js
// 1️⃣ Add the DEM source (used later for terrain, hillshade, etc.)
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png', // any raster-dem tile service
  tileSize: 256,
  // optional, but recommended for contour generation:
  // encoding: 'terrarium',   // default is 'mapbox' if omitted
  // maxzoom: 12,
});
```

> **Tip:** If you already have a `raster-dem` source in your style you can reuse it – you only need to pass the same URL/tileset to the `DemSource` constructor (see step 3).

---

## 3️⃣ Spin up a `DemSource` from `maplibre-contour`

```js
import mlcontour from 'maplibre-contour';

// Create a helper that knows how to fetch the DEM tiles
const demSource = new mlcontour.DemSource({
  // Must point to the same tiles you added above
  url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
  // Must match the encoding used by the DEM tiles
  encoding: 'terrarium',   // or 'mapbox', 'raw', etc.
  maxzoom: 12,             // highest zoom level you have DEM tiles for
  // Run the heavy work in a Web Worker (default = true)
  worker: true
});

// Register the helper with the MapLibre instance – this adds a custom
// protocol (`mlcontour://`) that the map can request as a vector source.
demSource.setupMaplibre(maplibregl);
```

### What `setupMaplibre` does
* Registers a **protocol handler** (`mlcontour://`) that returns on-the-fly generated vector tiles.
* Exposes a helper method `contourProtocolUrl()` that builds the URL for a vector source.

---

## 4️⃣ Add a **vector source** that points to the contour protocol

```js
// 2️⃣ Add a *virtual* vector source that will serve the generated contours
map.addSource('contours', {
  type: 'vector',
  // The URL is a special protocol that the library understands.
  // It contains all the parameters that control how contours are built.
  tiles: [
    demSource.contourProtocolUrl({
      // ── Contour spacing (in metres) per zoom level ───────────────────────
      //   You can give a single number (same spacing everywhere) or an
      //   object that maps zoom → [minor, major] intervals.
      thresholds: {
        // zoom: [minor, major] (minor = thin lines, major = thick / labelled)
        11: [200, 1000],
        12: [100, 500],
        14: [50, 200],
        // any zoom not listed falls back to the nearest lower entry
      },

      // ── Property names that will appear on each contour feature ────────
      elevationKey: 'ele',   // numeric elevation (m) of the line
      levelKey:     'level', // 1 = major contour, 0 = minor

      // ── Optional: give the source a stable name (useful for debugging) ──
      contourLayer: 'contours'   // appears as the source-layer name
    })
  ],
  // The vector tiles are generated on-the-fly, so we can set a generous maxzoom.
  maxzoom: 15
});
```

**Explanation of the options**

| Option | What it does |
|--------|--------------|
| `thresholds` | Controls the contour interval. You can supply a single number (`thresholds: 100`) for a uniform 100 m spacing, or an object that varies by zoom (recommended for readability). |
| `elevationKey` | Name of the numeric property that holds the elevation value. Use this in a label layer (`['get', 'ele']`). |
| `levelKey` | Name of the property that tells you whether the line is a *major* contour (`1`) or a *minor* one (`0`). Handy for styling different widths or adding labels only to majors. |
| `contourLayer` | The name of the **source-layer** that will appear inside the vector source. You’ll reference this in `source-layer` when adding a layer. |

---

## 5️⃣ Style the contour lines (and optional labels)

```js
// 3️⃣ Add the line layer that draws the contours
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-lines',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',   // matches the `contourLayer` above
  paint: {
    // Thin minor lines, thicker major lines
    'line-width': [
      'match',
      ['get', 'level'],
      1, 1.5,   // major contour
      0, 0.8,   // minor contour
      0.5      // fallback
    ],
    'line-color': [
      'match',
      ['get', 'level'],
      1, '#222222',   // dark major
      0, '#777777',   // lighter minor
      '#555555'       // fallback
    ],
    'line-opacity': 0.8
  }
});

// Optional: label the major contours
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-labels',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',
  filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1],   // only major contours
  layout: {
    'text-field': ['format',
      ['get', 'ele'],
      { 'font-scale': 0.9 },
      '\n',
      { 'text-font': ['Open Sans Regular'] }
    ],
    'text-size': 12,
    'text-font': ['Open Sans Bold'],
    'symbol-placement': 'line',
    'text-allow-overlap': false,
    'text-ignore-placement': false,
    'text-offset': [0, 0.5]
  },
  paint: {
    'text-color': '#222222',
    'text-halo-color': '#ffffff',
    'text-halo-width': 1
  }
});
```

**Result:** As you pan/zoom the map, the worker generates vector tiles on demand, and the line/symbol layers render them just like any other vector data.

---

## 6️⃣ Full Minimal Example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – Runtime Contours</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <script type="module">
    import mlcontour from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-contour@latest/dist/maplibre-contour.esm.js';

    const map = new maplibregl.Map({
      container: 'map',
      style: {
        version: 8,
        sources: {},
        layers: []   // we’ll add everything programmatically
      },
      center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
      zoom: 12,
      pitch: 45,
      bearing: -17
    });

    map.on('load', () => {
      // ---- 1️⃣ DEM source (used for terrain, hillshade, etc.) ----
      map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
        type: 'raster-dem',
        url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
        tileSize: 256,
        maxzoom: 12
      });

      // ---- 2️⃣ Set up the contour helper -----------------------
      const demSource = new mlcontour.DemSource({
        url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
        encoding: 'terrarium',
        maxzoom: 12,
        worker: true
      });
      demSource.setupMaplibre(maplibregl);

      // ---- 3️⃣ Virtual vector source for contours ---------------
      map.addSource('contours', {
        type: 'vector',
        tiles: [
          demSource.contourProtocolUrl({
            thresholds: { 11: [200, 1000], 12: [100, 500], 14: [50, 200] },
            elevationKey: 'ele',
            levelKey: 'level',
            contourLayer: 'contours'
          })
        ],
        maxzoom: 15
      });

      // ---- 4️⃣ Contour line layer -------------------------------
      map.addLayer({
        id: 'contour-lines',
        type: 'line',
        source: 'contours',
        'source-layer': 'contours',
        paint: {
          'line-width': [
            'match', ['get', 'level'], 1, 1.5, 0, 0.8, 0.5
          ],
          'line-color': [
            'match', ['get', 'level'], 1, '#222', 0, '#777', '#555'
          ],
          'line-opacity': 0.8
        }
      });

      // ---- 5️⃣ Optional label layer -----------------------------
      map.addLayer({
        id: 'contour-labels',
        type: 'symbol',
        source: 'contours',
        'source-layer': 'contours',
        filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1],
        layout: {
          'text-field': ['format', ['get', 'ele'], { 'font-scale': 0.9 }],
          'text-size': 12,
          'symbol-placement': 'line',
          'text-font': ['Open Sans Bold']
        },
        paint: {
          'text-color': '#222',
          'text-halo-color': '#fff',
          'text-halo-width': 1
        }
      });

      // ---- 6️⃣ (Optional) 3-D terrain to see the relief -------
      map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1.5 });
      map.setSky({
        'sky-color': '#88ccee',
        'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
        'horizon-color': '#fff',
        'fog-color': '#cceeff',
        'fog-ground-blend': 0.3
      });
    });
  </script>
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the page, pan/zoom, and you’ll see contour lines appear instantly—no pre-generated vector tiles required.

---

## 7️⃣ Things to Watch Out For

| Issue | Why it happens | Fix / Recommendation |
|-------|----------------|----------------------|
| **No contours appear** | The DEM tiles are not reachable, or the `encoding` is wrong. | Open the DEM URL in the browser, verify it returns an image. Check the `encoding` (`terrarium`, `mapbox`, `raw`). |
| **Contours look jagged / noisy** | DEM resolution is low or the `maxzoom` of the DEM source is too low. | Use higher-resolution DEM tiles (e.g., 12 → 13 zoom) or increase `maxzoom` in the `DemSource` config

## Yes – you can draw contour lines **directly from a `raster-dem` source at runtime**  
MapLibre GL JS does not have a built-in “contour” layer type, but the **`maplibre-contour`** library does the heavy lifting for you:

* It reads the same DEM tiles you already use for hillshade / 3-D terrain.  
* In a Web-Worker it extracts elevation values, runs a marching-squares algorithm and emits **vector tiles** that contain the contour geometry.  
* Those vector tiles are then added to the map like any other `vector` source and styled with a normal `line` (or `symbol`) layer.

Below is a step-by-step guide, a complete working example, and a few gotchas to keep in mind.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the library

```bash
# npm / yarn / pnpm
npm install maplibre-contour   # or: yarn add maplibre-contour
```

> The package ships a tiny Web-Worker (`worker.js`) that does the DEM → contour conversion.  
> If you are using a bundler that doesn’t automatically copy the worker file, you may need to expose it (e.g. `public/worker.js`).

---

## 2️⃣ Create the DEM source (the same one you already use)

```js
// A raster-dem source – can be Terrarium, Mapbox, or any supported encoding
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png', // <-- your DEM tiles
  tileSize: 256,
  // optional, but recommended for contour accuracy:
  // encoding: 'terrarium' | 'mapbox' | 'raw' (default is mapbox)
  // maxzoom: 12,
});
```

> **Why keep it as a `raster-dem` source?**  
> The same source can be reused for hillshade, `color-relief`, 3-D terrain **and** the contour generator, so you only download each DEM tile once.

---

## 3️⃣ Spin up a `DemSource` from `maplibre-contour`

```js
import mlcontour from 'maplibre-contour';

// 1️⃣ Create the helper that knows how to read your DEM tiles
const demSource = new mlcontour.DemSource({
  // The URL pattern must match the raster-dem source above
  url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
  // Must match the DEM encoding you used (default = 'mapbox')
  encoding: 'terrarium',          // ← change if you use a different encoding
  maxzoom: 12,                    // highest zoom you expect to request
  // Set to true to run the heavy work in a Web-Worker (recommended)
  worker: true,
});

// 2️⃣ Register the helper with the MapLibre instance
demSource.setupMaplibre(maplibregl);
```

### What the helper does

| Step | What happens |
|------|--------------|
| **Tile fetch** | The same DEM tiles are requested (cached by the browser). |
| **Decode** | The raw elevation values are decoded according to `encoding`. |
| **Contour generation** | For each tile a marching-squares pass creates isolines at the elevations you ask for. |
| **Vector tile packaging** | The isolines are packed into a tiny vector-tile blob that can be consumed by MapLibre. |
| **Protocol URL** | `demSource.contourProtocolUrl(opts)` returns a custom `contour://…` URL that the map treats as a normal vector source. |

---

## 4️⃣ Add a *virtual* vector source that points to the contour protocol

```js
// Choose the contour intervals you want at each zoom level.
// The object keys are zoom numbers, the values are [minor, major] intervals (in metres).
const thresholds = {
  11: [200, 1000],   // at z=11: minor every 200 m, major every 1000 m
  12: [100, 500],
  13: [50, 250],
  14: [25, 100],
  15: [10, 50],
};

map.addSource('contours', {
  type: 'vector',
  // The URL is a *protocol* that the DemSource registers.
  // It will be resolved on-the-fly to vector tiles containing the isolines.
  tiles: [
    demSource.contourProtocolUrl({
      thresholds,          // ← our interval map
      elevationKey: 'ele', // property name for the elevation value (default = 'ele')
      levelKey: 'level',   // property name for “major” vs “minor” (default = 'level')
      // Optional: give the source a name – useful for debugging
      contourLayer: 'contours',
    })
  ],
  // The vector source can be queried up to a higher zoom than the DEM itself,
  // because the library will up-sample the DEM if needed.
  maxzoom: 15,
});
```

> **Why a custom protocol?**  
> `contour://…` is not a real HTTP URL; the `DemSource` intercepts it, runs the worker, and returns a vector-tile blob.  
> This means you never have to host a separate contour tileset.

---

## 5️⃣ Style the contour lines

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-lines',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',   // the name we gave in `contourProtocolUrl`
  paint: {
    // Fade the lines a little so they don’t dominate the base map
    'line-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.6)',
    // Thicker line for “major” contours (level === 1)
    'line-width': [
      'match',
      ['get', 'level'],
      1,  // major
      1.5,
      0.6 // minor
    ],
    // Optional: dash pattern for minor contours
    'line-dasharray': [
      'match',
      ['get', 'level'],
      1, [2, 2], // major = solid
      [1, 2]    // minor = dashed
    ]
  }
});
```

### Adding labels (optional)

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-labels',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',
  layout: {
    'symbol-placement': 'line',
    'text-field': ['to-string', ['get', 'ele']], // elevation value
    'text-font': ['Open Sans Regular', 'Arial Unicode MS Regular'],
    'text-size': 10,
    'text-allow-overlap': false,
    'text-ignore-placement': false,
    'text-rotate-alignment': 'map',
    'text-pitch-alignment': 'viewport'
  },
  paint: {
    'text-color': '#222',
    'text-halo-color': '#fff',
    'text-halo-width': 1
  }
});
```

