---

## 7️⃣ Things to Keep in Mind

| Issue | Why it matters | How to address |
|-------|----------------|----------------|
| **DEM encoding mismatch** | `maplibre-contour` must decode the same numeric format you used for the DEM source (`mapbox`, `terrarium`, or `raw`). | Set `encoding` in both the `raster-dem` source **and** the `DemSource` options. |
| **Performance on low-end devices** | Contour generation is CPU-intensive (marching-squares + vector-tile packing). | • Keep `maxzoom` as low as possible (e.g., 12-13). <br>• Use a Web-Worker (`worker: true`). <br>• Limit the number of zoom levels in `thresholds`. |
| **Tile-cache duplication** | The DEM tiles are fetched twice (once for terrain/hillshade, once for contours) unless the browser cache is shared. | Ensure both sources use the *exact same* URL template; the browser will reuse the cached tiles. |
| **Contour density** | Too many intervals → many vertices → slower rendering. | Choose sensible `thresholds` per zoom; increase intervals at higher zooms, decrease at low zooms. |
| **Label clutter** | Every contour line gets a label by default. | Filter the label layer to only major contours (`['==', ['get', 'level'], 1]`) or use `text-max-angle`/`text-allow-overlap` to thin them out. |
| **Styling limits** | Only `line` and `symbol` layers can consume the generated vector source. | If you need fill-based shading (e.g., hypsometric tint) you still use a `color-relief` layer in parallel. |
| **Browser support** | The library uses `fetch` + `Response.arrayBuffer()` + `Worker`. Works in all modern browsers (Chrome, Edge, Firefox, Safari ≥ 14). | For older browsers you’d need a polyfill or fall back to pre-generated contour tiles. |

---

## 8️⃣ When to Prefer Pre-Generated Cont
